<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adoptionism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Spanish Adoptionism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adoptionism is the Christian doctrine that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, in his human nature, was the adopted rather than the natural Son of God. In late eighth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it was taught by Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elipandus">Elipandus of Toledo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Felix of Urgell, and attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatus-of-liebana">Beatus of Liébana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>. It was condemned by the councils of Regensburg in 792 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 794, and the controversy helped separate the Asturian church from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> under Muslim rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adoptionism.jpg" alt="Adoptionism" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Albani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Albani_-_Baptism_of_Christ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
